<%= lato_page_head "Documentation" %>

<h2>How to use Lato Settings</h2>

<p>
  <code>lato_settings</code> adds an application settings section to a Lato admin panel.
  Use it when admins need to edit values such as site name, feature flags, limits, labels,
  dates, or other configuration values without changing code.
</p>

<h3>Installation</h3>

<p>Requires Rails 8.1.3.1 or newer in the 8.1 series and Lato 3.25.0 or newer.
Existing applications must run <code>rails lato:install:migrations</code> and <code>rails db:migrate</code>
before deployment. Previous sessions are invalidated.</p>

<p>Values, keys, and labels are displayed as plain text. HTML is escaped, including existing saved settings.</p>

<p>Add Lato Settings to the application Gemfile:</p>

<pre><code>gem "lato"
gem "lato_settings"</code></pre>

<p>Install the engine and run migrations:</p>

<pre><code>bundle
rails lato:install:migrations
rails lato_settings:install:migrations
rails db:migrate</code></pre>

<p>
  <code>rails lato_settings:install:application</code> exists for consistency with other Lato engines but does nothing: it is not required.
</p>

<p>Mount the engine in <code>config/routes.rb</code>:</p>

<pre><code>Rails.application.routes.draw do
  mount LatoSettings::Engine =&gt; "/lato-settings"

  # ...
end</code></pre>

<p>Import styles in <code>app/assets/stylesheets/application.scss</code>:</p>

<pre><code>@import "lato_settings/application";</code></pre>

<p>Import JavaScript in <code>app/javascript/application.js</code>:</p>

<pre><code>import "lato_settings/application"</code></pre>

<h3>Permissions</h3>

<p>
  Users must be logged into Lato and must have Lato Settings admin permission enabled.
</p>

<pre><code>user = Lato::User.find_by(email: "admin@example.com")
user.update!(lato_settings_admin: true)</code></pre>

<h3>What admins can do</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Create settings.</li>
  <li>Edit setting values, labels, and descriptions (the key and type cannot be changed after creation).</li>
  <li>Delete optional settings.</li>
  <li>Read labels and descriptions to understand each setting purpose; the list shows type and required badge.</li>
  <li>Use type-specific inputs for text, numbers, dates, and select values.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Reading settings in application code</h3>

<p>
  Read values with <code>LatoSettings.get(key, default = nil)</code>. The key is case-insensitive.
  If the setting does not exist, or its stored value can't be converted to its type, the method returns the default value.
</p>

<pre><code>site_name = LatoSettings.get("site_name", "Default site")
items_per_page = LatoSettings.get("items_per_page", 25)
launch_date = LatoSettings.get("launch_date")</code></pre>

<div class="alert alert-info mt-3">
  <p class="mb-0">
    Prefer <code>LatoSettings.get</code> in new code.
  </p>
</div>

<h3>Supported setting types</h3>

<div class="table-responsive">
  <table class="table table-striped align-middle">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Type</th>
        <th>Use for</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>string</code></td>
        <td>Short text values.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>text</code></td>
        <td>Long text values.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>integer</code></td>
        <td>Whole numbers.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>number</code></td>
        <td>Decimal numbers.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>date</code></td>
        <td>Date values.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>select</code></td>
        <td>One value chosen from a list.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<h3>Returned types</h3>

<ul>
  <li><code>string</code>, <code>text</code>: <code>String</code></li>
  <li><code>integer</code>: <code>Integer</code></li>
  <li><code>number</code>: <code>Float</code></li>
  <li><code>date</code>: <code>Date</code></li>
  <li><code>select</code>: <code>String</code>, one of the allowed values</li>
</ul>

<p>A value is required and is validated against its type (and options) when saved.</p>

<h3>Options</h3>

<p>
  Each setting can have an <code>options</code> hash. It can be set from seeds or the console only: the admin panel can't edit it.
</p>

<div class="table-responsive">
  <table class="table table-striped align-middle">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Option</th><th>Used by</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>min</code>, <code>max</code></td><td>integer, number, date</td><td>Inclusive bounds (dates as <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>).</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>step</code></td><td>integer, number</td><td>Value must be <code>min + n * step</code> (min defaults to 0).</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>values</code></td><td>select</td><td>Array of allowed values, compared as strings.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<pre><code>LatoSettings::Setting.find_or_create_by!(key: "items_per_page") do |setting|
  setting.label = "Items per page"
  setting.typology = :integer
  setting.value = 25
  setting.options = { min: 1, max: 100, step: 1 }
end

LatoSettings::Setting.find_or_create_by!(key: "theme") do |setting|
  setting.label = "Theme"
  setting.typology = :select
  setting.value = "light"
  setting.options = { values: %w[light dark] }
end</code></pre>

<h3>Caching</h3>

<p>
  Values are cached for 1 minute in <code>Rails.cache</code> and in a per-process memo.
  Saving or destroying a setting clears the cache. After changes that skip callbacks
  (<code>update_all</code>, raw SQL, other applications) call <code>LatoSettings.reset_cache</code>.
  In production with several processes or hosts, configure a shared cache store (Redis, Memcached, Solid Cache);
  with a per-process store, other processes see changes only after the 1 minute TTL.
</p>

<h3>Security</h3>

<p>
  Values are stored as plain text in the database and in the cache: do not store secrets such as API keys or passwords.
  The engine filters <code>setting.value</code> from request logs.
</p>

<h3>Initial settings</h3>

<p>
  Create important settings in seeds, then let admins edit values from the panel.
  Mark a setting as required when it must not be deleted (required settings can still be edited).
</p>

<pre><code>LatoSettings::Setting.find_or_create_by!(key: "site_name") do |setting|
  setting.value = "My application"
  setting.label = "Site name"
  setting.description = "Name shown in public pages"
  setting.typology = :string
  setting.required = true
end</code></pre>

<h3>Key format</h3>

<p>
  Use clear lowercase keys with underscores, such as <code>site_name</code>, <code>items_per_page</code>, or <code>launch_date</code>.
  Keys are normalized when created (<code>Site Name</code> becomes <code>site_name</code>; accents are transliterated and other characters become <code>_</code>)
  and cannot be changed afterwards.
</p>
